## Box 1. Applications of the Cardiac Microenvironment In Vitro
hPSCs offer an unprecedented opportunity to generate human myocardial cells from a renewable cell source for the in vitro and in vivo study of human cardiac physiology and pathophysiology. To date, a diverse set of clinically relevant cardiac diseases have been modeled in vitro using stem cell-derived CMs. These diseases include channelopathies [121,122], cardiomyopathies [123,124], and cardiac metabolic diseases [119,125]. While current studies have mostly focused on recapitulating monogenic cardiac disorders, infectious [126] and metabolic diseases with a cardiac phenotype [119] have also been successfully modeled in vitro. PSC biology also provides novel platforms for cardiac drug discovery and testing [127,128]. These efforts may prove powerful not only in the development of new medical therapies but also in our understanding of drug-induced arrhythmogenicity and cardiotoxicity [129].
In developing these platforms, an important question remains regarding how single CMs and myocardial constructs will be functionally evaluated. While gene expression is commonly used to assess myocardial differentiation and maturation, the extensive structure–function relationship in myocardial cells requires new approaches to delineate the functional characteristics of hPSC-CMs [130,131]. The hallmarks of myocardial function are the generation of an action potential on change of membrane potential, the influx of calcium ions, and the consequent cellular contraction and force generation. For a comprehensive assessment of myocardial function, all of these processes have to be simultaneously evaluated. While the membrane potential and variations of intracellular calcium ions are commonly imaged optically with smallmolecule fluorescent indicators [132], a major and exciting step towards reducing the adverse effects of these chemicals has been the development of hPSC lines stably expressing optogenetic sensors either individually [28,133] or combined [133]. Novel systems to precisely measure multiple physiologic features of myocardial function at the single-cell [30,134] or tissue [56,135] level have also been developed. The cardiovascular field is rapidly moving from a descriptive to a more mechanistic characterization of the cell biology of the myocardium. It will be exciting to see how these techniques will aid in mechanistically elucidating functional alterations in $\mathsf{h P S C}$ disease models and drug development platforms.
to generate cellular energy. Consequently, exposing hPSC-CMs to a more lipogenic milieu promoting fatty acid $\upbeta$ -oxidation improves their maturity and allows the study of adult-onset heart diseases like diabetic cardiomyopathy [119] and arrhythmogenic right ventricular dysplasia [120].
In conclusion, in vitro models using hPSC-CMs have the potential to reflect many complex features of the adult human heart. To accomplish this goal, it is necessary to engineer tissue that incorporates the key aspects of the 3D biology of the human heart including biochemical signaling, the biomechanical milieu of the native heart, and electrical signaling within the heart (see Outstanding Questions). Integrating these key features requires the control of multiple interrelated aspects of cardiac biology including cell–cell signaling, cell–ECM interaction, electrical coupling, and energy utilization.
